<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eric Bloodaxe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/954">954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Eric Haraldsson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eric Bloodaxe was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a> who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> briefly in the 930s before being driven out by his brother Haakon. He later ruled the Viking Kingdom of York twice, around 947–948 and 952–954. Expelled by the Northumbrians in 954, he was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stainmore">Stainmore</a>, and York passed permanently to the English kings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eric-bloodaxe.jpg" alt="Eric Bloodaxe" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: York Museums Trust, Rebecca Griffiths, 2020-04-08 15:29:56 · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eric_Bloodaxe_silver_penny;_struck_952-954_AD_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> describes Eric only as Harold's son, and Norwegian histories and Icelandic sagas identify him as a son of the Norwegian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a>. The sagas are of limited historical value. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla">Heimskringla</a> names his mother as Ragnhildr, daughter of Eric, king of South Jutland, and says he was fostered by the hersir Thórir son of Hróald. It also tells that at the age of twelve he began a career of piracy, harrying the Baltic coasts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The origin of his nickname Bloodaxe is uncertain. The sagas usually explain it as referring to his killing of half-brothers, while Fagrskinna ascribes it to his violent reputation as a raider.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla">Heimskringla</a>, Harald appointed his sons as client kings and intended Eric, his favourite, to inherit the throne. Eric killed his half-brothers Ragnvald and Bjørn Farmann and, when Harald died, defeated the combined forces of Olaf and Sigrød, ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from 932 to 934. His rule was reputedly harsh, and the nobility turned against him. His half-brother Haakon, who had been raised at the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a>, returned and ousted him, and Eric fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><p>The English sources show Eric as King of Northumbria more firmly, though they give scanty detail and a confused chronology. His first reign, from 947 or 948 to 948, followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eadred">Eadred</a>'s attempt to bring the Northumbrians under his control. He was driven out, and Amlaíb returned. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> reports that in 952 the Northumbrians drove out King Olaf and accepted Eric, son of Harold, and in 954 they expelled him again. Coins minted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> bear his name, in two types, with the moneyers Ingalger and Radulf. The Life of St Cathróe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> says that his wife at York was a relative of the saint, probably of British or Scottish descent, whereas Egils saga names her Gunnhild.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Flores historiarum of Roger of Wendover says that Eric was treacherously killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stainmore">Stainmore</a>, betrayed by Earl Oswulf, by Earl Maccus, together with his son Haeric and his brother Ragnald, and that King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eadred">Eadred</a> then ruled the region. Fagrskinna cites the Eiríksmál, a poem said to have been commissioned by his widow Gunnhild, in which Eric is welcomed to Valhöll by Odin. The Rey Cross on Stainmore, an Anglo-Scandinavian cross possibly of the 10th century, has been linked to the battle, though no evidence supports this. In the sagas Eric became a mixture of history, folklore and political propaganda, a powerful warrior who was cruel and unpopular as a ruler.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eric Bloodaxe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Viking kings, the last of them <b>Eric Bloodaxe</b>, ruled York at intervals until 954, and under Æthelred the Unready renewed Danish attacks led to the conquest of England by Sweyn Forkbeard in 1013 and by his son Cnut in 1016.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The last of them, <b>Eric Bloodaxe</b>, was expelled in 954, traditionally dying at Stainmore, and York and the rest of Northumbria were absorbed into the Kingdom of England, with the north governed by earls of the house of Bamburgh.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Aethelstan seized York in 927, but Norse kings such as Olaf Guthfrithson, Amlaib Cuaran and <b>Eric Bloodaxe</b> returned repeatedly.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-york">Kingdom of York</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stainmore">Stainmore</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.53,-2.26&amp;year=944" aria-label="Stainmore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4500 296 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Eric Bloodaxe"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4950 566h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4950 566h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=54.53,-2.26&amp;year=944">What was here in 944 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
